Perfect! ✅ Now let’s test that your sudoers configuration and directory permissions are working correctly. We’ll do this step by step.

1️⃣ Confirm group memberships
groups devops1
groups sec1
groups backend1


devops1 should be in: devops sudo (or whatever you set)

sec1 should be in: security

Backend/frontend users in their respective groups

2️⃣ Check sudo privileges
As DevOps user
su - devops1
sudo whoami


✅ Expected: root (will prompt for password since no NOPASSWD)

sudo ls /root


✅ Expected: allowed

sudo cp /etc/hosts /tmp/


✅ Expected: allowed

As Security user
su - sec1
sudo journalctl -xe


✅ Expected: works, no password prompt

sudo less /var/log/syslog
sudo tail /var/log/auth.log
sudo grep "error" /var/log/syslog


✅ Expected: all work, no password prompt

sudo ls /etc


❌ Expected: denied, because not allowed in sudoers

3️⃣ Check directory permissions
ls -ld /apps/backend /apps/frontend /staging /finance/reports


✅ Expected output similar to:

drwxrwx---  root backend   /apps/backend
drwxrwx---  root frontend  /apps/frontend
drwxrwx---  root devops     /staging
drwxrwx---  root finance   /finance/reports


drwxrwx--- → owner + group can read/write/execute, others no access

Setgid bit (chmod 2770) ensures new files inherit group

4️⃣ Test group write access
# As backend1
su - backend1
touch /apps/backend/testfile


✅ Expected: allowed

# As frontend1
touch /apps/backend/testfile


❌ Expected: denied, only group members can write

5️⃣ Optional: Test NOPASSWD
# As sec1
sudo journalctl -xe


Should not prompt for a password

Verify log entries appear

⚡ Summary

These commands confirm:

Users are in the correct groups

Sudo rules work exactly as defined

Directory ownership & permissions are enforced

NOPASSWD works for Security, password required for DevOps
